# motd (Message of the Day)

It’s a long-standing convention in networked systems (going back to Unix login banners and early MUDs).

Purpose: a short, human-readable string sent by the server at connection/handshake.

Typically contains a welcome line, server info, or a brief status/announcement.

It’s not required for protocol correctness; purely informational to the user.

In PR-2 handshake, motd might default to "Welcome to the Saskan Lands".

It shows up in the client CLI on success.

It lives in the payload of handshake.reply.

If empty/missing, client just prints a generic welcome.
